Given an array of integers `nums` and an integer `target`, return the indices of the two numbers that add up to `target`. Each input has exactly one solution, and you can't use the same element twice.

The brute-force approach checks every pair — O(n^2). The trick to O(n) is a hash map: as you scan once, for each number ask "have I already seen the value that would complete this pair?" instead of scanning ahead for it.

```ts
function twoSum(nums: number[], target: number): number[] {
  const seen = new Map<number, number>();
  for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
    const complement = target - nums[i];
    if (seen.has(complement)) {
      return [seen.get(complement)!, i];
    }
    seen.set(nums[i], i);
  }
  return [];
}
```

This pattern — trade space for time with a hash map to avoid a nested loop — shows up constantly: it's the same idea behind detecting duplicates, grouping anagrams, and counting pairs with a given difference.

Given a string containing only `()[]{}`, determine whether the brackets are properly matched and nested.

The key insight: the most recently opened bracket must be the first one closed — that's exactly a stack's Last-In-First-Out behavior. Push every opening bracket; on a closing bracket, pop and check it matches.

```ts
function isValid(s: string): boolean {
  const stack: string[] = [];
  const pairs: Record<string, string> = { ')': '(', ']': '[', '}': '{' };
  for (const char of s) {
    if (char in pairs) {
      if (stack.pop() !== pairs[char]) {
        return false;
      }
    } else {
      stack.push(char);
    }
  }
  return stack.length === 0;
}
```

Don't forget the final check: a string like `'(('` never fails inside the loop (nothing closes to mismatch), but it isn't valid — the stack must be empty at the end.

Given `prices[i]` = the stock price on day `i`, find the maximum profit from a single buy followed by a single sell (buy must happen before sell).

A one-pass greedy scan works: walk left to right, tracking the lowest price seen so far. At each day, ask "if I sold today, having bought at the lowest price so far, what's my profit?" and keep the best.

```ts
function maxProfit(prices: number[]): number {
  let minPrice = Infinity;
  let maxProfit = 0;
  for (const price of prices) {
    if (price < minPrice) {
      minPrice = price;
    } else if (price - minPrice > maxProfit) {
      maxProfit = price - minPrice;
    }
  }
  return maxProfit;
}
```

If prices only ever fall, no trade is profitable — the answer is 0, not a negative number, since you're always allowed to simply not trade.

Find the contiguous subarray with the largest sum. This is the canonical introduction to dynamic programming via Kadane's algorithm: at each element, decide whether extending the current subarray is still worthwhile, or whether starting fresh from this element beats carrying the old sum along.

```ts
function maxSubArray(nums: number[]): number {
  let currentSum = nums[0];
  let maxSum = nums[0];
  for (let i = 1; i < nums.length; i++) {
    currentSum = Math.max(nums[i], currentSum + nums[i]);
    maxSum = Math.max(maxSum, currentSum);
  }
  return maxSum;
}
```

A common mistake is initializing the running sum to 0 instead of `nums[0]` — that silently breaks on all-negative arrays, where the correct answer is the least-negative single element, not zero (the subarray must be non-empty).

You're climbing a staircase of `n` steps, and each move you can climb 1 or 2 steps. How many distinct ways are there to reach the top?

The number of ways to reach step `n` is the number of ways to reach step `n-1` (then take one more step) plus the number of ways to reach step `n-2` (then take two more) — the exact recurrence behind the Fibonacci sequence. A naive recursive solution recomputes the same sub-answers exponentially many times; tracking just the last two values iteratively gets it down to O(n) time and O(1) space.

```ts
function climbStairs(n: number): number {
  if (n <= 2) {
    return n;
  }
  let prev = 1;
  let curr = 2;
  for (let i = 3; i <= n; i++) {
    const next = prev + curr;
    prev = curr;
    curr = next;
  }
  return curr;
}
```

Reverse a singly linked list in place. The classic iterative technique walks the list once, flipping each node's `next` pointer to point backward instead of forward, while keeping track of the previous node.

```ts
class ListNode {
  value: number;
  next: ListNode | null = null;
  constructor(value: number) {
    this.value = value;
  }
}

function reverseList(head: ListNode | null): ListNode | null {
  let prev: ListNode | null = null;
  let current = head;
  while (current !== null) {
    const next = current.next;
    current.next = prev;
    prev = current;
    current = next;
  }
  return prev;
}
```

The critical detail: you must save `current.next` into a temporary variable *before* overwriting it — otherwise you lose your only reference to the rest of the list.
